<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oh My Darling, Clementine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">published <a href="/years/1884">1884</a>; based on a song printed in <a href="/years/1863">1863</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Oh My Darling, Clementine&quot; (Roud 9611, sometimes simply &quot;Clementine&quot;) is a traditional American Western folk ballad, tragic but sometimes comic, in trochaic metre. It is usually credited to Percy Montrose (or Montross), 1884, and sometimes to Barker Bradford, 1885.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Many versions of the song exist, but all centre on Clementine, the daughter of a miner forty-niner and the singer's lover. One day, while doing her chores, she trips, falls into a raging current and drowns, and her lover cannot save her because he cannot swim. Montross's version ends with the lines &quot;Though in life I used to hug her, now she's dead – I'll draw the line.&quot;</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Oliver Ditson &amp; Co of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> issued Percy Montrose's lyrics as sheet music in 1884, based on an earlier song, Down by the River Liv'd a Maiden, printed in 1863. The origin of the melody is unknown. In his book South from Granada Gerald Brenan claims that it came from an old Spanish ballad made popular by Mexican miners during the California Gold Rush, best known as the Romance del Conde Olinos o Niño. It is unclear who first recorded the song in English. The first version to reach the Billboard charts was Bing Crosby's of 14 June 1941, which briefly reached number 20, in an arrangement by Hal Hopper and John Scott Trotter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Later performers reworked it. Tom Lehrer sang variations in 1959 in the styles of Cole Porter, Mozart, bebop and Gilbert and Sullivan; Jan and Dean's Clementine, released on the Dore label in November 1959, reached 65 on the Billboard Hot 100; and Bobby Darin's version of 1960, with lyrics credited to Woody Harris, reached number 12 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. The song runs through John Ford's film My Darling Clementine of 1946, and members of the Western Writers of America chose it as one of the Top 100 Western songs of all time.</p><p>The tune travelled widely. In 1961 a group of Ituri pygmies sang to it when the ethnomusicologist Colin Turnbull asked for the oldest song they knew. It carries the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-new-year">Chinese New Year</a> song Xīnnián Hǎo, the Jewish New Year song Dip The Apple In The Honey, a Scandinavian student drinking song, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> lullaby and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoka">EOKA</a> song marking the start of the uprising against British rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> on 1 April 1955. In Animal Farm George Orwell likened the tune of Beasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to a mix of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-cucaracha">La Cucaracha</a> and Oh My Darling, Clementine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oh My Darling, Clementine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>Oh My Darling, Clementine</b>&quot; (Roud 9611, sometimes simply &quot;Clementine&quot;) is a traditional American Western folk ballad, tragic but sometimes comic, in trochaic metre.</blockquote><a href="/songs/oh-my-darling-clementine">Oh My Darling, Clementine</a></li></ul></section>
